            <ArticleTitle>Pharmacotherapy for end-stage coronary artery disease.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>207-13</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1517/14656560903439737</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="IMPORTANCE OF THE FIELD" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">Coronary artery disease remains the leading cause of mortality in the industrialized world. Despite advances in surgical and catheter-based interventions, a select number of patients remain with no options for invasive therapy. The goal of this review is to discuss the current status of pharmacotherapeutic interventions to treat end-stage coronary artery disease.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="AREAS COVERED IN THIS REVIEW" NlmCategory="METHODS">Literature review on the topic of therapeutic angiogenesis from 1980 to 2009.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="WHAT THE READER WILL GAIN" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Insight into current therapeutic strategies employed to manage end-stage coronary artery disease.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="TAKE HOME MESSAGE" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">A promising approach focuses on augmenting the endogenous angiogenic response to chronic myocardial ischemia via the use of growth factors.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
